 [F1] The selected quiver category is a finite-field hereditary Hall category by [[lem-finite-loopless-quiver-nilpotent-category-is-hereditary-finitary]]. Its simple Euler values symmetrize to the displayed Cartan matrix. The Green coproduct in [[def-green-hall-completion-and-coproduct]] makes each vertex simple primitive, since it has only the zero subobject and itself. The Green form is $([M],[N])_H=\delta_{[M],[N]}/|\operatorname{Aut}M|$, so its extension to finite real sums is positive definite. Its homogeneous adjunction is compatible with the primitive braided coproduct on words in vertex simples, with twist $\nu^{(\alpha,\beta)}$, vacuum norm $1$ and simple norms $1/(Q-1)$ ([[prop-green-hall-pairing-is-nondegenerate]], [[thm-green-hall-coproduct-is-a-twisted-bialgebra-map]]). Here the primitive assertion refers to the root-graded braided Hall coproduct, before adjoining the torus.
 
-[F2] The normalized generic half form $B_t$ has primitive tensor twist $t^{-(\alpha,\beta)}$ and simple norms $1$. Its divided-power values have only Gaussian-factorial denominators, and all ordinary-word values are Laurent polynomials ([[thm-the-negative-half-bilinear-form-is-symmetric-nondegenerate-and-integral]]).
+[F2] The actual generic symmetric half has the uniquely normalized homogeneous form $B_t$ defined by the matching-permutation formula on ordinary words. Its adjunction is primitive first-letter deletion with tensor twist $t^{-(\alpha,\beta)}$ and simple norms $1$. Ordinary-word values are Laurent polynomials; divided-power products have only finite products of symmetric quantum factorials as denominators. In each degree, the finite ordinary-word Gram matrix $G_\alpha(t)$ satisfies $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb Q(c)}G_\alpha(c)\le p_\alpha$ at every positive real $c$, where $p_\alpha$ is the classical PBW rank. These assertions, including actual Serre-quotient descent, are proved in [[lem-finite-serre-word-form-and-classical-rank-bound]].
 
-[F3] $U_A^-$ is a graded algebra generated by divided powers $F_i^{(n)}=F_i^n/[n]_t!$, and its components are finite free of the classical PBW ranks ([[thm-integral-triangular-decomposition-for-the-lusztig-form]]).
+[F3] The integral half $U_A^-$ is the actual $A$-subalgebra of the generic half generated by $F_i^{(n)}=F_i^n/[n]_t!$. This is its definition in [[def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form]]. Quantum factorials have nonzero evaluations at $c=\nu^{-1}>0$; their symmetric quantum-integer factors are positive sums $c^{n-1}+c^{n-3}+\cdots+c^{1-n}$.
 
 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Let $\mathcal T$ be the free $A$-algebra on formal symbols $X_{i,n}$ with $i\in I,n\ge1$, graded by $\deg X_{i,n}=n\alpha_i$. Send $X_{i,n}$ to $F_i^{(n)}$ in the generic half, and denote its image algebra $U_A^-$ by [F3]. Define a second map on $\mathcal T$ by specializing $t\mapsto\nu^{-1}$ and sending $X_{i,n}$ to $u_i^n/[n]_{\nu^{-1}}!$ in $C_K$. All denominators are nonzero because $\nu>1$. The latter map has image $C_K$, since $X_{i,1}$ maps to $u_i$. [given, F3, algebra]
+1.1 Let $\mathcal T$ be the free $A$-algebra on $X_{i,n}$, $i\in I,n\ge1$, with degree $n\alpha_i$. The map $X_{i,n}\mapsto F_i^{(n)}$ has image the actual integral half $U_A^-$. Define $\Phi:\mathcal T\to C_K$ by coefficient evaluation $t\mapsto c=\nu^{-1}$ and $X_{i,n}\mapsto u_i^n/[n]_c!$. The denominator evaluations are nonzero by [F3]. Its image includes every $u_i$, so its $K$-linear span is $C_K$. [given, F3, algebra]
 
-2.1 For homogeneous $P,R\in\mathcal T$ of degree $\alpha$, the Hall pairing of their second images is $(\Phi(P),\Phi(R))_H=(Q-1)^{-\operatorname{ht}\alpha}\left.B_t(P(F),R(F))\right|_{t=\nu^{-1}}$. Indeed first take ordinary words: both sides are determined by the same primitive coproduct and tensor twist, the same simple pairings and the vacuum value, by peeling a first letter through the adjunction. The twists coincide because $(\nu^{-1})^{-(\alpha,\beta)}=\nu^{(\alpha,\beta)}$, and the height factor changes each generator norm from $1$ to $1/(Q-1)$. The formula then extends to divided words by their scalar denominators and to $A$-linear combinations by bilinearity and evaluation of coefficients. All expressions are evaluable by [F2]. Unequal degrees pair to zero on both sides. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+2.1 For ordinary words $w,z$ of degree $\alpha$ and height $h$, the primitive Hall coproduct and Green adjunction evaluate their Hall pairing by deleting successive letters. Deleting a letter at position $r$ crosses the preceding letters that remain in the second tensor factor and contributes $\nu^{\sum_{s<r}a_{i,j_s}}$. Each generator pairing contributes $1/(Q-1)$. Thus repeated deletion gives exactly $(w(u),z(u))_H=(Q-1)^{-h}B_t(w(F),z(F))|_{t=c}$, since $c^{-a}=\nu^a$. Equivalently both sides have the same first-letter recursion, vacuum value and generator norms after the height rescaling. By dividing by the evaluated factorials and taking finite linear combinations, for homogeneous $P,R\in\mathcal T$ one obtains $$ (\Phi(P),\Phi(R))_H=(Q-1)^{-\operatorname{ht}\alpha}B_t(P(F),R(F))|_{t=c}. $$ Every evaluation is legitimate by the actual form's localization bound in [F2]. Unequal degrees give zero on both sides. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
 
-3.1 If $P$ belongs to the kernel of $\mathcal T\to U_A^-$ and is homogeneous, then $P(F)=0$ in the generic half. Therefore $B_t(P(F),P(F))=0$ as a rational function. Step 2.1 makes $(\Phi(P),\Phi(P))_H=0$, so positivity in [F1] gives $\Phi(P)=0$. The kernel is graded, and applying this to its finitely many homogeneous components proves that the entire kernel is killed. Thus $\Phi$ factors through an $A$-linear algebra map $U_A^-\to C_K$ (with $A$ acting by the stated evaluation). The tensor universal property gives the asserted map $\Psi$, which is surjective by step 1.1. This factorization used no nondegeneracy assertion and no finite-parameter Serre-presentation theorem. Formula 2.1 passes through the factorization and gives the stated isometry. Surjectivity and [F3] give the PBW upper bound. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
+3.1 If homogeneous $P$ is in the actual kernel of $\mathcal T\to U_A^-$, then $P(F)=0$ as an element of the actual generic half. Since [F2] has constructed a well-defined bilinear form on that actual half, its norm $B_t(P(F),P(F))$ is the zero rational function. Step 2.1 and positive definiteness of the real Green form imply $\Phi(P)=0$. The kernel is graded, so applying this to its finitely many homogeneous components kills every kernel element. The map $\Phi$ consequently factors through an $A$-algebra map $U_A^-\to C_K$, and extension of scalars gives an algebra map whose image contains $K$ and every $u_i$, hence the surjection $\Psi:K\otimes_{A,t\mapsto c}U_A^-\to C_K$ asserted in the Statement. The evaluated form on $U_A^-$ is $A$-balanced and induces a $K$-bilinear form on its scalar extension. Step 2.1 passes through the quotient and scalar extension, proving the stated form-preservation assertion, with the factor $(Q-1)^{-\operatorname{ht}\alpha}$. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 Fix $\alpha$. The finite set of ordinary words $W_\alpha$ spans $C_K[\alpha]$, since $C_K$ is generated by the homogeneous simple classes. Let $E:K^{W_\alpha}\to C_K[\alpha]$ be word evaluation. Its pullback Green Gram matrix is $(Q-1)^{-\operatorname{ht}\alpha}G_\alpha(c)$ by step 2.1. The kernel of this matrix is exactly $\ker E$: one inclusion is immediate; if $v$ is in the matrix kernel then $(E(v),E(v))_H=0$, and positivity in the real Hall space gives $E(v)=0$. All coefficients lie in the specified real field $K=\mathbb Q(c)=\mathbb Q(\nu)$. Finite rank-nullity therefore gives $$\dim_K C_K[\alpha]=\operatorname{rank}_K G_\alpha(c)\le p_\alpha.$$ The final inequality is [F2]. This proves the dimension consequence without asserting freeness of $U_A^-[\alpha]$, nondegeneracy of the generic or specialized form, or injectivity of any numerical Serre-presentation map. [F1, F2, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
 
